也就是让她见她的女儿，这真的很温馨。

156
00:06:55.390 --> 00:06:55.910
I really...
我真的……

157
00:06:55.910 --> 00:06:56.810
That was a good B story.
那是个很棒的B故事。

158
00:06:56.810 --> 00:06:58.190
It was very, very good ending.
结尾真的非常非常棒。

159
00:06:58.190 --> 00:06:58.670
These were the...
这些是……

160
00:06:58.670 --> 00:07:02.550
This was a rare instance where the A story and B story didn't overlap that much.
这是一个罕见的例子，主线故事和副线故事并没有那么多的交集。

161
00:07:02.550 --> 00:07:06.770
Although there was kind of a trust overlap, and there's something thematically there
尽管有一种信任上的交集，并且在主题上有些东西

162
00:07:06.770 --> 00:07:07.410
about both.
是两者兼有的。

163
00:07:07.670 --> 00:07:09.570
But both of these are really good.
但这两个都真的很棒。

164
00:07:09.870 --> 00:07:14.710
You know, this was not everyone on the same storyline, but this was, I think, A story,
你知道，这不是所有人都挤在同一条剧情线上，但我觉得，主线故事，

165
00:07:14.810 --> 00:07:18.410
B story, equally juicy, interesting, valid issues.
副线故事，同样精彩、有趣、有价值的问题。

166
00:07:18.810 --> 00:07:23.390
You know, just going back to what you asked Marshall and Lily to do, that kind of sweaty
你知道的，回到你要求马歇尔和莉莉去做的事，那种让人捏一把汗的

167
00:07:23.390 --> 00:07:23.830
move.
举动。

168
00:07:24.030 --> 00:07:24.350
Yeah.
是的。

169
00:07:24.490 --> 00:07:28.310
If you're on a show for nine years, like every once in a while, your character has to be
如果你在一部剧里演了九年，就像偶尔地，你的角色必须当

170
00:07:28.310 --> 00:07:28.790
the dummy.
那个傻瓜。

171
00:07:29.670 --> 00:07:30.030
Yes.
是的。

172
00:07:30.030 --> 00:07:31.030
Like every once in a while, your character...
就像偶尔地，你的角色……

173
00:07:31.030 --> 00:07:31.630
There's no way around it.
这是没办法的事。

174
00:07:31.630 --> 00:07:37.730
It has to be the one who said, like, foot in the mouth, like, I'm the idiot who propels
必须是那个说出类似于祸从口出的话的人，也就是那个推动

175
00:07:37.730 --> 00:07:38.870
the plot forward in...
剧情发展的笨蛋……

176
00:07:38.870 --> 00:07:40.370
Shakespeare writes those scenes.
莎士比亚也会写这样的场景。

177
00:07:40.730 --> 00:07:40.910
Yes.
是的。

178
00:07:40.910 --> 00:07:41.470
You know what I mean?
你知道我的意思吗？

179
00:07:41.590 --> 00:07:42.370
Like, you have...
就像，你有……

180
00:07:42.950 --> 00:07:44.250
That's a half of Shakespeare.
那占了莎士比亚的一半。

181
00:07:44.650 --> 00:07:45.330
Yeah, yeah.
对，对。

182
00:07:46.050 --> 00:07:48.170
Is someone saying the wrong thing and overhearing it?
就是有人说错话然后被偷听到了？

183
00:07:48.210 --> 00:07:50.230
Yeah, and also, like, there's a lot of...
对，而且还有，有很多……

184
00:07:50.230 --> 00:07:53.230
When you watch, like, Shakespeare, like, there's a lot of implausibility.
当你观看，比如，莎士比亚的作品时，有很多不合情理的地方。

185
00:07:53.450 --> 00:07:54.510
You're like, just go with it.
你会觉得，别纠结了，接受吧。

186
00:07:54.590 --> 00:07:55.090
Just go with it.
接受就好。

187
00:07:55.270 --> 00:07:56.330
There's so much you have to go with.
有太多你需要接受的设定了。

188
00:08:01.630 --> 00:08:04.210
It's exactly the same, and you can't tell the difference between them.
这完全一样，你根本分不清它们的区别。

189
00:08:05.230 --> 00:08:09.190
I feel no shame in the occasional sweaty moment, because Shakespeare had them too.
对于偶尔出现的尴尬时刻我毫无羞愧，因为莎士比亚也有过。

190
00:08:09.670 --> 00:08:10.390
I mean, it's cool.
我是说，这很酷。

191
00:08:10.530 --> 00:08:14.210
I love talking to you about this stuff, Craig, because I almost feel like I'm, like, fascinated
克雷格，我很高兴能和你聊这些，因为我感觉自己就像是对一块手表

192
00:08:14.210 --> 00:08:19.350
by a watch, and I open up the thing and get to see the gears, you know?
充满了好奇，打开表壳就能看到里面的齿轮，你知道吗？

193
00:08:19.490 --> 00:08:24.610
Because I'm really, I'm really, I feel like as a writer, my strong suits are, like, plot.
因为我真的，我真的觉得作为一个编剧，我的强项在于剧情。

194
00:08:24.850 --> 00:08:25.510
I'm not plot.
我其实不擅长剧情。

195
00:08:25.670 --> 00:08:30.710
It's actually character and kind of the emotional beats, but, like, there's so much, and you
其实是人物塑造和情感节奏，但是，有很多，而且

196
00:08:30.710 --> 00:08:34.470
guys are brilliant at that on this show, but you also have, and I thought this about
你们在这部剧里真的很擅长这一点，但你们也有，而且我觉得你的小说

197
00:08:34.470 --> 00:08:35.330
your novel, too.
也是这样的。

198
00:08:35.970 --> 00:08:39.170
I really thought, you're really good at plot mechanics.
我真的觉得，你非常擅长剧情构思的技巧。

199
00:08:39.370 --> 00:08:39.850
Oh, thank you.
哦，谢谢你。

200
00:08:39.910 --> 00:08:42.470
In a way that I feel like I'm still kind of a student of.
在这方面我觉得我还在学习中。

201
00:08:42.470 --> 00:08:43.590
No, thank you.
不用了，谢谢。

202
00:08:43.750 --> 00:08:49.410
I mean, that's very nice, and also sometimes you just have to go, this one's a little sweaty,
我是说，这很好，而且有时你不得不接受，这一段有点勉强，

203
00:08:49.490 --> 00:08:50.110
but we need it.
但我们需要它。

204
00:08:50.490 --> 00:08:50.710
Yeah.
是的。

205
00:08:50.990 --> 00:08:54.670
And you have to be like, this is how the sausage is made at this moment, and it's also.
你必须觉得，这就是目前香肠的制作方式，而且它也是。

206
00:08:54.870 --> 00:08:56.070
You know when Ted was the dummy?
你知道泰德当替身的时候吗？

207
00:08:56.850 --> 00:08:58.810
Oh, many times, but what's possible?
哦，很多次，但有什么可能的？

208
00:08:58.910 --> 00:09:01.070
I need some ointment or something.
我需要一些药膏之类的东西。

209
00:09:02.070 --> 00:09:02.850
Wait a minute.
等一下。

210
00:09:03.010 --> 00:09:04.830
What is this, Spanish massage oil?
这是什么，西班牙按摩油？

211
00:09:07.850 --> 00:09:12.550
For every creative high point, there's always going to be, uh, the circuits are jammed.
对于每一个创意高潮，总会伴随着，呃，电路堵塞。

212
00:09:14.610 --> 00:09:16.070
That's what you get on New Year's Eve.
这就是跨年夜会遇到的情况。

213
00:09:16.090 --> 00:09:17.490
What do you expect on New Year's Eve?
你在跨年夜期望什么呢？

214
00:09:17.510 --> 00:09:18.410
You know, New Year's Eve.
你知道的，跨年夜。

215
00:09:18.630 --> 00:09:19.110
What?
什么？

216
00:09:19.690 --> 00:09:22.030
And for every, yeah, every, you're right.
而且对于每一个，对，每一个，你是对的。

217
00:09:22.230 --> 00:09:27.830
And every now and again, poor Jason Segel or Lily Aldrin has to, like, let it slip and
而且时不时地，可怜的杰森·席格尔或莉莉·奥尔德里奇不得不，比如，说漏嘴然后

218
00:09:27.830 --> 00:09:28.970
say the stupid thing.
说出那个蠢话。

219
00:09:29.310 --> 00:09:30.710
And all of a sudden, it's Three's Company.
突然之间，这就成了《三人行》。

220
00:09:31.170 --> 00:09:32.530
All of a sudden, it's Jack Ritter.
突然之间，这就成了杰克·里特。

221
00:09:33.210 --> 00:09:33.550
This is it.
就是这样。

222
00:09:33.690 --> 00:09:36.650
I like to make references just specifically for the 20-somethings.
我喜欢专门为二十几岁的人做一些梗。

223
00:09:39.790 --> 00:09:42.530
And it's, you know, it's just comical misunderstanding theater.
而且，你知道，这简直就是滑稽的误会戏剧。

224
00:09:42.730 --> 00:09:44.090
I blurted out the wrong thing.
我脱口而出了错误的话。

225
00:09:44.250 --> 00:09:46.870
I'm going to try to suck it back into my mouth and make it even worse.
我打算把它吸回嘴里，却让事情变得更糟。

226
00:09:47.010 --> 00:09:48.050
And you're just, it's vaudeville.
然后你就会发现，这就是杂耍。

227
00:09:48.090 --> 00:09:52.490
But, you know, you know, it's also like you guys were doing, you guys were doing something
但是，你知道，你知道，这也就像你们在做的，你们当时在做一件

228
00:09:52.490 --> 00:09:57.090
I think really interesting, which was you were trying to exist within the conventions
我认为非常有趣的事，那就是你们试图在一种形式的传统中生存，

229
00:09:57.090 --> 00:10:04.210
of a form, which at that point had 50 years of, like, convention, you know, and you were
这种形式在当时已经有50年的传统了，你知道，而且你们

230
00:10:04.210 --> 00:10:11.270
also trying to rattle the cage that you were in and bring in Pulp Fiction, independent film,
还试图摇晃你们身处的那座笼子，引入《低俗小说》、独立电影、

231
00:10:11.570 --> 00:10:13.370
great albums you loved.
你们喜爱的伟大专辑。

232
00:10:13.530 --> 00:10:15.130
Like, you were really trying to do something new.
就像，你们真的在尝试做一些新鲜的事情。

233
00:10:15.230 --> 00:10:21.050
And I think that the tension within that created a great classic show.
而且我认为其中蕴含的张力创造了一部伟大的经典剧集。

234
00:10:21.330 --> 00:10:21.490
Yeah.
是的。

235
00:10:21.630 --> 00:10:25.450
But at the same time, there were moments when the show had to be Three's Company.
但与此同时，有些时候这部剧不得不成为《三人行》。

236
00:10:25.450 --> 00:10:31.910
You see the DNA of the sitcom sometimes because it is that it's also trying to very much not
你有时能看到情景喜剧的基因，因为它一方面是那样，另一方面在很大程度上又极力

237
00:10:31.910 --> 00:10:33.450
be that a great deal of time.
不想成为那样。

238
00:10:33.590 --> 00:10:38.850
And occasionally you're just like this particular gear of this machine has to be this thing.
偶尔你就会觉得，这台机器的这个特定齿轮必须是这个东西。

239
00:10:38.850 --> 00:10:42.890
It has to be the sweaty, very, like, ungraceful.
它必须是那种汗流浃背的、非常不优雅的。

240
00:10:43.190 --> 00:10:45.950
This is the meat grinder part of the operation.
这是整个运作过程中的绞肉机部分。

241
00:10:46.250 --> 00:10:47.990
And it's going to send the story forward.
它将推动故事向前发展。

242
00:10:48.150 --> 00:10:48.970
We're going to get somewhere good.
我们会去到某个不错的地方。

243
00:10:48.970 --> 00:10:50.650
But we need that moment to happen.
但我们需要那个时刻发生。

244
00:10:50.910 --> 00:10:52.570
And you guys were good at selling them.
而且你们很擅长把它们演好。

245
00:10:52.750 --> 00:10:58.930
Siegel and Allie did the Lord's work here, taking the hit of having these sweaty moments.
席格尔和艾莉在这里做了上帝般的工作，承受了这些尴尬时刻的打击。

246
00:10:59.190 --> 00:11:03.270
Every night, the sweaty moments were traded around the cast at times.
每天晚上，这些尴尬时刻会在演员之间轮流分担。

247
00:11:03.510 --> 00:11:05.770
And in this time, it landed on them.
而这一次，落在了他们头上。

248
00:11:05.770 --> 00:11:14.690
And I wrote you this little ditty to sing to you in New York City.
我为你写了这首小曲，要在纽约市唱给你听。

249
00:11:15.250 --> 00:11:16.890
We'll be right back.
我们马上回来。

250
00:11:24.820 --> 00:11:26.700
And now, back to the show.
现在，言归正传。

251
00:11:28.520 --> 00:11:30.080
This episode's a fun one.
这一集很有意思。

252
00:11:30.200 --> 00:11:34.960
It's exploring Barney's search to replace Ted, which is just kind of heartbreaking in a way.
它探讨了巴尼寻找泰德替代者的过程，从某种角度来说，这其实挺让人心碎的。

253
00:11:35.100 --> 00:11:35.920
You know, it's so sad.
你知道的，这太悲伤了。

254
00:11:36.060 --> 00:11:37.140
Barney's just out in the wilderness.
巴尼就像是在荒野中独自流浪。

255
00:11:37.400 --> 00:11:41.580
But it also, you get this moment of really deeper intimacy between Ted and Stella.
但与此同时，泰德和斯特拉之间也产生了一些真正更深层次的亲密感。

256
00:11:41.580 --> 00:11:44.440
And we loved Sarah Chalk on the show so much.
而且我们太喜欢莎拉·乔克在这个节目里的表现了。

257
00:11:44.540 --> 00:11:51.920
We wanted that relationship to deepen fast, knowing that we had very little access to putting her on the TV show.
我们希望那段关系能迅速加深，因为我们知道能让她参与节目的机会非常少。

258
00:11:52.060 --> 00:11:56.800
And what I mean by that is, she was on another TV show called Scrubs at the same time.
我的意思是，她当时还在拍另一部叫《实习医生风云》的电视剧。

259
00:11:57.020 --> 00:12:01.780
And we had to make her appearances on How Much Your Mother Count.
我们必须充分利用她每一次出现在《老爸老妈浪漫史》里的机会。

260
00:12:02.240 --> 00:12:05.160
So that's why it's been five years since she's had sex.
所以这就是为什么她已经五年没有过性生活了。

261
00:12:05.260 --> 00:12:11.440
You guys lured her over by giving her an absolutely different role to play, which was a dermatologiSt.
你们把她引诱过来，是给了她一个完全不同的角色，也就是个皮肤科医生。

262
00:12:11.580 --> 00:12:12.180
Yeah, yeah.
对，没错。

263
00:12:12.500 --> 00:12:15.480
A different type of doctor is a huge stretch for Sarah.
对莎拉来说，演另一种类型的医生可真是一个巨大的挑战。

264
00:12:15.680 --> 00:12:22.000
I mean, she had to slightly cover up the name tag that said her character's name from Scrubs.
我的意思是，她不得不稍微遮住那个写着她在《实习医生风云》里角色名字的名牌。

265
00:12:22.200 --> 00:12:24.680
She was literally wearing the same fucking lab coat.
她当时穿着的简直就是同一件该死的白大褂。

266
00:12:24.680 --> 00:12:27.260
It's also like, bring your white doctor coat.
这就好比是，带上你白色的医生外套吧。

267
00:12:27.780 --> 00:12:28.820
You're costuming yourself.
你自己带戏服。

268
00:12:28.960 --> 00:12:29.880
She's so kind.
她人太好了。

269
00:12:29.880 --> 00:12:34.040
But I tried to give her the opportunity to be like, yeah, that was a bummer, guys.
但我试着给她一个机会，让她觉得，好吧，这事儿确实挺扫兴的，伙计们。

270
00:12:34.080 --> 00:12:36.540
When she was on the show a couple episodes ago, I was like, I'm sorry about that.
当她几集前来到剧组时，我就说，真是不好意思啊。

271
00:12:36.540 --> 00:12:40.100
Because I think she could have been like, these motherfuckers really making me be a doctor.
因为我觉得她本可以抱怨说，这群混蛋居然真让我来当医生。

272
00:12:40.100 --> 00:12:41.680
No, you know why you wouldn't as an actor?
不，你知道作为一个演员，你为什么不会这么想吗？

273
00:12:41.940 --> 00:12:43.940
Because you're like, I know this coat fits well.
因为你会觉得，我知道这件衣服很合身。

274
00:12:44.260 --> 00:12:45.420
I look good in this coat.
我穿这件外套很好看。

275
00:12:45.600 --> 00:12:47.640
This coat, this coat, I feel comfortable in.
这件外套，这件外套，让我觉得很舒服。

276
00:12:47.660 --> 00:12:49.160
It's like, it's tailored to me.
就好像是，它是专门为我量身定制的。

277
00:12:49.380 --> 00:12:55.940
But we, I like that this episode, something I forgot about this episode is that it really deepens Ted and Stella in a hurry.
但我，我喜欢这一集的一点是——关于这一集我差点忘了的一点——它确实让泰德和斯特拉的关系飞速加深。

278
00:12:55.940 --> 00:13:05.080
And we were, I think, trying to trick everyone and maybe ourselves into, like, I think we had to agree that, yeah, yeah, we know we don't have that much access to Sarah.
而且我想当时我们也在试图欺骗所有人，甚至可能也在欺骗我们自己，就好像，我觉得我们不得不承认，对，对，我们知道我们没那么多机会让莎拉出场。

279
00:13:05.220 --> 00:13:09.100
We're not going to build too much on that, knowing in the back of our heads that we were doing the exact opposite.
我们不会在这个上面铺垫太多，但其实我们心里很清楚，我们做的是完全相反的事情。

280
00:13:09.340 --> 00:13:16.540
That we were trying to deepen Ted and Stella's connection and build it in to be a huge structural support of season four.
我们是在努力加深泰德和斯特拉的联系，并将其打造成第四季的一个巨大的结构支撑。

281
00:13:16.900 --> 00:13:19.940
Season four is very much about Stella and Ted.
第四季很大程度上都是关于斯特拉和泰德的。

282
00:13:20.140 --> 00:13:22.680
And it gets to some really intense places with the two of them.
而且他们两个人的关系会发展到一些非常激烈的地步。

283
00:13:22.740 --> 00:13:23.840
I won't jump ahead too much here.
我在这里就不剧透太多了。

284
00:13:24.120 --> 00:13:25.300
But this episode helps.
但这一集很有帮助。

